\nseveral of these congruences and also introduced additional divisibility relations.\n\nThis article contributes further to the topic by extending various congruences\n\nfrom [2] using elementary q-series identities. We also supply elementary proofs for\n\nseveral results from [2] that were not addressed in [11] and [19].\n\nWe ﬁrst analyze the pair (ℓ, µ) = (2,3) and deduce the following internal con-\n\ngruence modulo 3.\n\nTheorem 1.1. For all n≥0, we have\n\nR 2,3(27 n)≡R 2,3(3 n) (mod 3).\n\nIn the following corollary, we establish inﬁnitely many families of congruences\n\nmodulo 6.\n\nCorollary 1.2. For all β≥1 and n≥0, we have\n\n(1) R 2,33 β(3 n+ 2)≡0 (mod 6).\n\nThe congruence (10) given in [2, Theorem 4.1],\n\nR 2,3(9 n+ 6) ≡0 (mod 6),\n\nis a special case of (1) with β= 1, and an elementary proof was given in [11,\n\nTheorem 3.1].\n\nNext, we consider the pair (ℓ, µ) = (4,3) and prove the following new congru-\n\nences.\n\nTheorem 1.3. For all n≥1, we have\n\n(2) R 4,3(2 n)≡0 (mod 4).\n\nIf n≥1 is not an odd square, then we have\n\n(3) R 4,3(2 n)≡0 (mod 8).\n\nEXTENDING RECENT CONGRUENCE RESULTS ON (ℓ, µ)-REGULAR OVERPARTITIONS 3\n\nWe note that Theorem 1.3 implies the congruences (16) and (17) given in [19,\n\nTheorem 3.1]:\n\nR 4,3(4 n+ 2) ≡0 (mod 4),\n\nR 4,3(8 n+ 4) ≡0 (mod 8).\n\nFinally, we investigate the pair (ℓ, µ) = (4,9). An elementary proof of R 4,9(4 n)≡\n\n0 (mod 3) was given in [19, Theorem 4.1]; however, we extend this congruence to\n\nthe modulus 12 and provide a diﬀerent elementary proof here.\n\nTheorem 1.4. For n≥1, we have\n\n(4) R 4,9(4 n)≡0 (mod 12).\n\nTheorem 1.4 implies the congruences (16) and (17) given in [2, Theorem 4.1]:\n\nR 4,9(8 n+ 4) ≡0 (mod 12),\n\nR 4,9(12 n+ 4) ≡0 (mod 12).\n\nIt also conﬁrms the following conjecture proposed in [2].\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nS. Chandankumar - arXiv Combinatorics (https://www.arxiv.symmetricfunctions.com/author/s-chandankumar)\nciteturn7search7 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; Extending Recent Congruence Results on $t$-Schur overpartitions ... In this paper, we extend their work and establish several new congruence relations for $t$-Schur overpartitions.\n# arXiv++ Combinatorics\n\nBrowse math.CO papers from arXiv\n\n[Input: Search by title, author, BibTeX key, or arXiv URL...] [Button: Search]\n\n## Papers by S. Chandankumar\n\n2 paper(s) by this author · All BibTeX\n\n2025-08-22\n\nK. C. Ajeyakashi, H. S. Sumanth Bharadwaj, S. Chandankumar\n\n# • INFO • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\nExtending Recent Congruence Results on $t$-Schur overpartitions\n\nRecently, Nadji and Ahmia~\\cite{nadji2021} introduced the notion of $t$-Schur overpartitions and investigated their combinatorial and arithmetic properties. In this paper, we extend their work and establish several new congruence relations for $t$-Schur overpartitions. For example, for all $n \\ge 0$ we prove \\[ \\overline{S_9}(24n+23)\\equiv 0 \\pmod{32}. \\]\n\noverpartition schur\n\n2025-05-18\n\nYashas N., C. Shivashankar, S. Chandankumar\n\n# • INFO • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\nArithmetic properties of $(\\ell,m)$-regular colored partitions\n\nLet $b^{k}_{\\ell,m}(n)$ denotes the number of $k-$colored partitions of $n$ into parts that are not multiples of $\\ell$ or $m$. We establish several congruence relations for $b_{\\ell,m}(n)$. For instance, for any nonnegative integer $n$ $$b^{2}_{4,5}(8n+7) \\equiv 0 \\pmod{40}.$$\n\ncolored partition partition\n\nMaintained by Per Alexandersson, per@symmetricfunctions.com\n\nBibTeX [Button: ×]--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHaiyang Wang - arXiv Combinatorics (https://arxiv.symmetricfunctions.com/author/haiyang-wang)\nciteturn7search8 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; overpartition representation ... Extending Recent Congruence Results on $(\\ell,μ)$-Regular Overpartitions ... In this paper, we substantially extend several of their results and establish infinitely many families of new congruences.\n\noverpartition\n\n2025-05-28\n\nBishnu Paudel, James A. Sellers, Haiyang Wang\n\n# • INFO • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\nExtending Recent Congruence Results on $(\\ell,μ)$-Regular Overpartitions\n\nRecently, Alanazi, Munagi, and Saikia employed the theory of modular forms to investigate the arithmetic properties of the function $\\overline{R_{\\ell,μ}}(n)$, which enumerates the overpartitions of $n$ where no part is divisible by either $\\ell$ or $μ$, for various integer pairs $(\\ell, μ)$. In this paper, we substantially extend several of their results and establish infinitely many families of new congruences. Our proofs are entirely elementary, relying solely on classical $q$-series manipulations and dissection formulas.\n\noverpartition\n\n2025-03-16\n\nBishnu Paudel, James A. Sellers, Haiyang Wang\n\n# • INFO • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\nExtending recent work of Nath, Saikia, and Sarma on $k$-tuple $\\ell$-regular partitions\n\nLet $T_{\\ell,k}(n)$ denote the number of $\\ell$-regular $k$-tuple partitions of $n$. In a recent work, Nath, Saikia, and Sarma derived several families of congruences for $T_{\\ell,k}(n)$, with particular emphasis on the cases $T_{2,3}(n)$ and $T_{4,3}(n)$. In the concluding remarks of their paper, they conjectured that $T_{2,3}(n)$ satisfies an infinite set of congruences modulo 6. In this paper, we confirm their conjecture by proving a much more general result using elementary $q$-series techniques. We also present new families of congruences satisfied by $T_{\\ell,k}(n)$.\n\npartition--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nComputer Science (Duluth) - Research output - Experts@Minnesota (https://experts.umn.edu/en/organisations/computer-science-duluth/publications/?type=%2Fdk%2Fatira%2Fpure%2Fresearchoutput%2Fresearchoutputtypes%2Fcontributiontojournal%2Farticle&type=%2Fdk%2Fatira%2Fpure%2Fresearchoutput%2Fresearchoutputtypes%2Fcontributionto